ore and as you can  see 14% because we do a pretty good job  in this country of treating hypertension  there's only 14% who took no medications  55% about half two are one or two and  31% well three and ten who took three or  more blood blood pressure medications  and basically what we did we know that  those who take medications are different  than those that don't  so accounting for all the factors that  we know of that may make that may make  people who take medications different  than those that do or those who take  more different than those who take less  accounting for all of those factors what  we found was comparing to those people  who took no anti hypertensive  medications those that took one and two  had no effect no decrease in the  likelihood of having a stroke or a heart  attack in the three years of follow-up  nor those who took three or more very  different from what we found in the  clinical trials but what we did find is  that again people who took no blood  pressure medications adjusting for all  those factors making them look more  alike 40% increased risk in a series  fall injuries such as a hip fracture or  a traumatic brain injury with one to two  medications and by almost a 30 percent  increase in very serious injuries for  three or more medications interestingly  enough there was a decreased risk in  death one of the things were postulating  perhaps it doesn't decrease the  occurrence of a cardiovascular event but  maybe improves the mortality for those  who have an event and it's an area that  we're still exploring but what we do  summarize is that in this population  of real older adults if you will the  larger amounts of antihypertensive  medications seem to be associated with a  decreased risk of death but an increased  risk of very very serious injuries such  as hip fractures and head injuries and  at least in our population no effect on  cardiovascular events are perhaps only a  modest decrease compared to particularly  in strokes com